*A shared scope for functions

  Variables give a set of functions the same convenience that a single method gives its statements.

  A single method has one local scope.  Each statement can use any variable declared above it.  Set a value
  on one line and every later line can use it.  The state accumulates in scope as the method runs.

  Composing the same work from separate functions would normally lose that scope.  A function sees only its
  own parameters.  With return values a function hands one value to the next.  To carry several accumulated
  values it has to bundle them into a type.  As the flow grows, so does the number of these carrier types,
  one for each combination of state in flight.  The types model nothing.  They exist only to move state
  between functions.

  Variables restore the shared scope.  A function publishes a value with <<<Out>>>.  Any later function
  reads it with <<<@Val>>>.  The set of live variables is the shared scope, so each function reads any
  earlier value it needs.  Each function is like a block of code in the single method, with its variables
  still available to the functions that follow.  There are no carrier types.

  It also improves on the single method.  In a method a block's use of the surrounding variables is
  implicit, and the block cannot be reused on its own.  A function instead declares the variables it reads
  and the variables it writes.  Its inputs and outputs are explicit, so the function is reusable wherever
  those variables are in scope.

  So variables give the flexibility of a shared local scope with the discipline of an explicit, reusable
  contract on each function.



